1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,465
Are you still paying for last Christmas?

2
00:00:02,885 --> 00:00:05,543
Let's make sure this year doesn't follow you into next fall.

3
00:00:05,963 --> 00:00:11,152
Because holiday spending on a credit card isn't really a gift. It's a loan, with interest.

4
00:00:11,922 --> 00:00:17,545
So here's a holiday budget that fits the cash you actually have, in five steps, with real numbers.

5
00:00:18,315 --> 00:00:20,661
Step one. Start with the cash, not the list.

6
00:00:21,081 --> 00:00:26,091
Say you have $600, between savings and the goal money from your December paychecks.

7
00:00:26,511 --> 00:00:29,119
That number is the budget. Not your credit limit.

8
00:00:29,889 --> 00:00:34,360
Step two. Write the real list. Every person, and every cost that isn't a gift.

9
00:00:34,780 --> 00:00:35,863
Here's the gift list.

10
00:00:36,023 --> 00:00:37,262
Partner, $150.

11
00:00:37,422 --> 00:00:38,937
Two kids, $120 each.

12
00:00:39,097 --> 00:00:40,550
Both parents, $60 each.

13
00:00:40,710 --> 00:00:42,192
Three siblings, $40 each.

14
00:00:42,352 --> 00:00:43,794
Four friends, $20 each.

15
00:00:43,954 --> 00:00:45,960
And the work gift swap, $25.

16
00:00:46,380 --> 00:00:48,526
Then, the costs nobody puts on the list.

17
00:00:48,686 --> 00:00:50,493
Gas to visit family, $90.

18
00:00:50,653 --> 00:00:52,292
Hosting dinner, $140.

19
00:00:52,452 --> 00:00:54,269
Wrapping and cards, $35.

20
00:00:54,429 --> 00:00:55,343
Shipping, $40.

21
00:00:55,763 --> 00:01:01,043
Add it up, and the real total is $1,040. That's $440 more than the cash.

22
00:01:01,813 --> 00:01:03,558
Step three. Don't borrow the gap.

23
00:01:03,978 --> 00:01:11,259
Put the gap on a credit card at 24%, pay $50 a month, and it takes 10 months and about $49 in interest.

24
00:01:11,679 --> 00:01:14,134
You'd still be paying for this Christmas in October.

25
00:01:14,554 --> 00:01:20,285
So here's the rule. If you can't pay it off by the January statement, it's not a gift. It's a loan.

26
00:01:21,055 --> 00:01:23,681
Step four. Trim by priority, not by guilt.

27
00:01:24,101 --> 00:01:25,139
Start with the gifts.

28
00:01:25,559 --> 00:01:29,758
Partner goes from $150 to $100. The kids, $200 total.

29
00:01:30,178 --> 00:01:31,386
Parents, $30 each.

30
00:01:31,806 --> 00:01:37,337
Three siblings? Suggest a Secret Santa, and you buy one $40 gift instead of three.

31
00:01:37,757 --> 00:01:41,379
Friends get homemade cookies, about $20 of ingredients.

32
00:01:41,799 --> 00:01:46,559
And skip the work swap this year. Gifts go from $735 to $420.

33
00:01:46,979 --> 00:01:48,137
Then the hidden costs.

34
00:01:48,557 --> 00:01:50,822
Gas stays. You still want to see your family.

35
00:01:51,242 --> 00:01:54,083
Make dinner a potluck, and hosting drops to $60.

36
00:01:54,503 --> 00:02:00,093
Plain paper and twine instead of fancy wrap: $10. Order early and ship less: $20.

37
00:02:00,513 --> 00:02:03,264
Hidden costs go from $305 to $180.

38
00:02:03,684 --> 00:02:10,734
$420 plus $180 is $600. Exactly the cash you have, and nothing follows you into next year.

39
00:02:11,504 --> 00:02:13,951
Step five. Fix next year, in January.

40
00:02:14,371 --> 00:02:22,753
Start a holiday sinking fund with your first January paycheck. $600 over 24 paychecks is $25 each.

41
00:02:23,173 --> 00:02:28,483
Next December, you start with the cash already there. Episode three walks through exactly how.

42
00:02:29,254 --> 00:02:34,047
The Paycheck Planner in the description has a surprise fund tab built for exactly this.

43
00:02:34,467 --> 00:02:37,222
Subscribe for more money math, with real numbers.
